CloudBurrow patch 21 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1188).

The bug: a TIMESTAMP with no time zone was read in America/Los_Angeles,
the analyzer's default zone, where BigQuery reads it in UTC ("If no time
zone is specified, the default time zone, UTC, is used", BigQuery's
timestamp type docs). Measured through CloudBurrow's front, on the
emulator built with patches 0001 to 0017: TIMESTAMP '2020-01-01',
CAST('2020-01-01' AS TIMESTAMP), CAST(DATE '2020-01-01' AS TIMESTAMP),
SAFE_CAST('2020-01-01' AS TIMESTAMP) and the bounds of
RANGE<TIMESTAMP> '[2020-01-01, 2020-02-01)' were each 2020-01-01 08:00 UTC,
and CAST(TIMESTAMP '2020-01-01 05:00:00+00' AS STRING) was
"2019-12-31 21:00:00-08". TIMESTAMP '2020-01-01 00:00:00', TIMESTAMP(...)
and a CAST of a column were already UTC. go-googlesql has no binding that
makes a TimeZone, so the analyzer's default cannot be set.

The patch extends applyNaiveTimestampUTC, the rewrite that gives a
TIMESTAMP literal with no zone "+00:00" before the analyzer reads it:
- it read a date alone's "-01" as an offset and left it; a date alone is
  now its midnight in UTC;
- the bounds of a RANGE<TIMESTAMP> literal with no zone, and the string
  of CAST('<value>' AS TIMESTAMP) or SAFE_CAST, get the same.
Turning off the analyzer's folding of a CAST of a literal
(SetFoldLiteralCast(false)) was tried and dropped: the run time's CAST
then refused a named zone ("2020-01-01 00:00:00 America/New_York") and
wrote a TIMESTAMP as RFC 3339 text. Still in the default zone: a CAST of a
DATE or DATETIME literal to TIMESTAMP, and a CAST of a TIMESTAMP literal
to STRING.

